The Artist's Daughter

Rani's talent for painting was extraordinary, but her uncle insisted art wasn't a "suitable career for girls." Her father, a traditional man, surprised everyone by enrolling her in art classes.

"My daughter's happiness is more important than society's expectations," he declared at a family gathering. When relatives pressured him about getting her married instead, he stood firm.

Years later, Rani's paintings are displayed in galleries across India. She opens her own art school, teaching young girls to paint their dreams into reality.